      "latency_ms": 5727.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 1203,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, forming the molecule **H₂O**. It is a fundamental and ubiquitous substance on Earth, essential for all known forms of life. Here's a breakdown of its key characteristics:\n\n### **Chemical Composition**\n- **Molecular Structure**: Water is a polar molecule, with oxygen (more electronegative) pulling electrons toward itself, creating a partial negative charge on the oxygen and partial positive charges on the hydrogens. This polarity enables hydrogen bonding between water molecules, which underpins many of its unique properties.\n\n### **Physical Properties**\n- **States of Matter**: Water exists as a **liquid** at room temperature, but it can also transition into **solid (ice)** at 0°C (32°F) and **gas (water vapor)** at 100°C (212°F) under standard atmospheric pressure.\n- **Unique Properties**: \n  - **High Specific Heat**: It absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and environments.\n  - **Cohesion & Adhesion**: Molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), enabling processes like capillary action in plants.\n  - **Solvent Ability**: Often called the \"universal solvent,\" it dissolves many substances, facilitating chemical reactions in biological and environmental systems.\n\n### **Biological Importance**\n- **Life-Sustaining**: Water is a primary component of cells, blood, and bodily fluids. It transports nutrients, removes waste, and participates in metabolic reactions.\n- **Hydration**: Essential for maintaining cellular function, temperature regulation, and overall health in living organisms.\n\n### **Environmental Role**\n- **Hydrological Cycle**: Water cycles through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff, shaping Earth's climate and ecosystems.\n- **Erosion & Landscaping**: It carves rivers, lakes, and canyons over time, influencing geological features.\n\n### **Other Key Traits**\n- **Tasteless, Odorless, and Colorless**: Pure water has no distinct taste or smell. In large quantities, it appears blue due to light absorption.\n- **Abundance**: While common on Earth, water is relatively rare in the universe compared to hydrogen and oxygen individually.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple yet extraordinary molecule whose unique properties make it indispensable for life, environmental processes, and countless scientific and industrial applications."
    },
